Swiftpm may invoke clang, not clang++, to compile C++. Our cc-wrapper also
doesn't pick up the arguments that enable C++ compilation in this case. Patch
swiftpm to properly invoke clang++.

diff --git a/Sources/Build/BuildDescription/ClangModuleBuildDescription.swift b/Sources/Build/BuildDescription/ClangModuleBuildDescription.swift
index 987a586..1049bce 100644
--- a/Sources/Build/BuildDescription/ClangModuleBuildDescription.swift
+++ b/Sources/Build/BuildDescription/ClangModuleBuildDescription.swift
@@ -456,7 +456,13 @@ public final class ClangModuleBuildDescription {
 
         args += ["-c", path.source.pathString, "-o", path.object.pathString]
 
-        let clangCompiler = try buildParameters.toolchain.getClangCompiler().pathString
+        // `isCXX` here is the *per-file* value computed above from the source
+        // extension, not `clangTarget.isCXX`, which is true for the whole module
+        // as soon as it contains a single C++ file. A module may mix the two
+        // (swift-build's `SWBCSupport` has `CLibclang.cpp` and
+        // `CLibRemarksHelper.c`), and compiling a `.c` file with `clang++` gives
+        // its symbols C++ linkage, so nothing can link against them afterwards.
+        let clangCompiler = try buildParameters.toolchain.getClangCompiler(isCXX: isCXX).pathString
         args.insert(clangCompiler, at: 0)
         return args
     }
